[13:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'impossible for the selfie stick or the clamp to touch the frame. You\'re safe." Here, I\'ll show you. Watch this. No matter where I turn my handlebars, you know, it\'s not going to collide. The other thought you might have is, "Why'

[14:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t you just use the integrated handlebar mount which places the camera right here?" It\'s still in front of you, and you can change the camera angle in post. Yes, you can change the angle, but you can\'t change the position. As you can see, I hit some bumps. Oh, yes, stiff carbon fiber selfie stick'

[14:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in action. It changes the position from down here to up here, so you\'re looking into my eyes, not my double chin. I think we\'d all rather look into these. Let me get back into the shadow where I'

[14:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'belong. One of the things I do with this clamp is I make sure to put the clamp at the end of the bars where the plug is, so the plug is helping to protect uh the carbon handlebars. You never want to clamp this like up here or up here because then you\'re putting pressure on'

[14:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the handlebars themselves. Because this plug is here, that\'s kind of supporting the carbon, so you can clamp down on this. The last position I like to do is shooting it out kind of back towards the back near the derailleur. All right, so'

[14:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this one\'s super cool, but you do have to be careful about your distance between here cuz uh when you\'re turning right, you could slam the selfie stick in there. So, proceed with caution. As we learned earlier, I do not shoot it'

[15:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directly out to the side. Do this instead. So, as long as you push it out just a little bit, you\'ll still have enough room to pedal, and it\'s going to be back near your rear wheel. You\'re going to get a cool shot. You\'re going to look faster than you actually are.'

