[16:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but before that, a pro feature\'s no good if the battery just dies within 5 minutes. So, today, myself, Sam, and Steven, we\'re going to ride until the battery dies, which will probably be somewhere up in those'

[16:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mountains you can\'t see in the fog somewhere. And we\'re setting it to i-Log. It\'s 24 frames a second. We\'re shooting 8K. This is like max settings. All right, you ready, Sam?'

[17:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Sam, do you have any guesses on how long it\'ll last? &gt;&gt; 70 minutes. &gt;&gt; Steven, any guesses? &gt;&gt; About an hour. &gt;&gt; Are you a morning person, Steven, or you &gt;&gt; I used to be. &gt;&gt; Where\'s my key cam? All right, I\'m looking at the camera. We'

[17:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are at hour and 30 minutes and 5 seconds since I started recording. It looks like the battery is about halfway. It\'s a perfect time to talk to you about log. Your camera nerd friends may have told you before about why log is so'

[17:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'important, but if they haven\'t, let me be your camera nerd friend right now. Off to my right is the sun. It\'s really bright. Down here on the ground is my shadow, that\'s very dark. The difference between the brightest point of the image and the'

[17:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'darkest point of the image is the dynamic range of that image. A camera that has a big dynamic range can capture the brightest point and the darkest point more accurately than a camera that has a small dynamic range.'

[17:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s why sometimes when you have the sun behind you, you take a picture and the sun looks fine, but then you\'re like just a complete shadow or vice versa, you look fine, but the sun\'s all blown out and'

[18:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sky looks white instead of blue. That\'s cuz your camera didn\'t have good dynamic range. This camera, when you shoot in log, has 13 steps of dynamic range. Now, when you shoot in log at first, it'

[18:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'may look like your image is not great, it\'s actually washed out. And that\'s because of how log captures the image. It\'s a much flatter image because it\'s containing more dynamic'

[18:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'range, more data in the highlights and in the shadows than a normally processed images. The trick to using log footage is to then bring it into your editing program'

[18:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and apply a LUT. That stands for a lookup table. And that\'s going to convert your log image into ta-da a normal image. The other advantage is that when you\'re color grading, if you'

[18:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to get really creative and push the image really hard, you [snorts] have not only more dynamic range, but this is 10-bit I log, so you actually have more colors to push. So, things don\'t break apart like when you\'re looking at the blue'

[19:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sky, there\'s actually more blue colors available in 10-bit than there are in 8-bit. Now, you may be saying, "Mitch, this is nice, but I honestly I just want to post videos to my Instagram." I can summarize everything with this. 10-bit I log look good.'

[19:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; How good? &gt;&gt; Real good. Here\'s an image from the last generation camera, the X5, in a similar setup. And this is the 10-bit I log image from the X6. Look how much cleaner and clearer the details are, the edges,'

[19:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the colors, roll off between gradations. &gt;&gt; You still have to look out for things like stitching errors, which I didn\'t do a great job here as you can see, but this is by far the best image I\'ve ever seen coming out of an Insta360 camera,'

[19:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it has me so excited. &gt;&gt; All right, we\'re 2 hours 10 minutes in. It looks like we\'ve used 2/3 of the'

